1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,330
When we say someone has been
put through the wringer,

2
00:00:02,440 --> 00:00:04,390
we are describing a real machine.

3
00:00:04,860 --> 00:00:08,020
The wringer was a pair of rollers
that squeezed water out of

4
00:00:08,060 --> 00:00:08,810
wet clothes.

5
00:00:09,160 --> 00:00:12,910
It was so rough that it popped
buttons and crushed careless fingers.

6
00:00:13,404 --> 00:00:16,254
It sat on top of an
early washing machine,

7
00:00:16,384 --> 00:00:20,014
and that machine was only
one step in a very long line.

8
00:00:20,604 --> 00:00:23,504
Inventors had been trying to build
a machine for washing clothes

9
00:00:23,544 --> 00:00:26,864
for more than two hundred years
before one worked well in an

10
00:00:26,904 --> 00:00:28,014
ordinary home.

11
00:00:28,362 --> 00:00:30,722
This is the story of
the washing machine,

12
00:00:30,902 --> 00:00:34,142
and why it was invented many
times before it was finished.

13
00:00:40,833 --> 00:00:41,663
Before machines,

14
00:00:42,053 --> 00:00:43,343
laundering meant soaking,

15
00:00:43,713 --> 00:00:46,903
beating, scrubbing and
rinsing every piece by hand.

16
00:00:47,213 --> 00:00:50,993
In the eighteen hundreds, a
household might heat water in a large

17
00:00:51,033 --> 00:00:54,463
copper tub and squeeze the
clothes through a wooden mangle,

18
00:00:54,753 --> 00:00:57,563
a pair of wooden rollers
worked by a big lever.

19
00:00:58,122 --> 00:01:00,612
The first washing
machine patents are old.

20
00:01:01,102 --> 00:01:04,962
In sixteen ninety-one, an English
inventor named John Tyzacke

21
00:01:05,002 --> 00:01:08,392
was granted a patent for a
washing and wringing machine.

22
00:01:08,852 --> 00:01:11,292
Almost nothing is known
about how it worked.

23
00:01:11,700 --> 00:01:15,900
In seventeen sixty-seven, a German
named Jacob Christian Schäffer

24
00:01:15,940 --> 00:01:18,910
published a design for a
rotary washing machine,

25
00:01:19,240 --> 00:01:21,270
and he is said to have
made sixty of them.

26
00:01:21,780 --> 00:01:25,540
In seventeen eighty-two, an Englishman
named Henry Sidgier patented

27
00:01:25,580 --> 00:01:29,290
a wooden barrel with rods
inside and a crank to turn it.

28
00:01:29,640 --> 00:01:32,530
Water flushed through the
rods as the barrel turned.

29
00:01:32,975 --> 00:01:36,735
An early American patent for a
washing machine is credited to

30
00:01:36,775 --> 00:01:38,475
a man named Nathaniel Briggs,

31
00:01:38,515 --> 00:01:40,565
in the seventeen nineties.

32
00:01:40,715 --> 00:01:42,425
We do not know what it looked like.

33
00:01:42,795 --> 00:01:46,115
The record is said to have been
lost when the Patent Office burned

34
00:01:46,155 --> 00:01:47,685
in eighteen thirty-six.

35
00:01:48,308 --> 00:01:50,558
We have no film of
any of these machines.

36
00:01:51,148 --> 00:01:53,778
The pictures in this
chapter are stand-ins:

37
00:01:53,868 --> 00:01:57,098
a nineteen fifteen film about
electricity in the home,

38
00:01:57,408 --> 00:01:59,838
a laundry scene from
nineteen twenty-seven,

39
00:02:00,108 --> 00:02:02,538
and some later machines
from a company film.

40
00:02:03,153 --> 00:02:06,663
For a long time, the machine and
the problem stayed far apart.

41
00:02:07,013 --> 00:02:09,543
The machines were
hand-cranked and hard work.

42
00:02:09,873 --> 00:02:11,583
Laundry stayed hard work too.

43
00:02:18,200 --> 00:02:21,860
In eighteen fifty-one, an American
named James King patented

44
00:02:21,900 --> 00:02:24,490
a hand-powered washing
machine with a drum.

45
00:02:25,140 --> 00:02:28,600
Sources describe it as the first
American patent for a washer

46
00:02:28,640 --> 00:02:29,610
of that kind.

47
00:02:30,060 --> 00:02:33,970
Later in the century, other
inventors added a reversing action,

48
00:02:34,120 --> 00:02:36,610
so the drum turned
one way and then back,

49
00:02:36,820 --> 00:02:38,970
which stopped the
clothes from bunching up.

50
00:02:39,455 --> 00:02:42,645
Many of these machines were
built for commercial laundries,

51
00:02:42,835 --> 00:02:43,845
not for homes.

52
00:02:44,155 --> 00:02:47,455
What was missing was a source of
power that a family could use.

53
00:02:47,495 --> 00:02:49,965
And then electricity
reached the house.

54
00:02:50,628 --> 00:02:52,288
In nineteen oh six, two brothers,

55
00:02:52,328 --> 00:02:55,848
Neil and Edward Hurley, formed
the Hurley Machine Company in

56
00:02:55,888 --> 00:02:56,558
Chicago.

57
00:02:57,028 --> 00:03:00,298
Their first product was an
electric washer called the Thor,

58
00:03:00,568 --> 00:03:03,058
designed by an engineer named Alva J.

59
00:03:03,508 --> 00:03:04,058
Fisher.

60
00:03:04,488 --> 00:03:06,918
The patent was issued
on the ninth of August,

61
00:03:07,128 --> 00:03:10,228
nineteen ten, three years after
the machine was first made.

62
00:03:10,268 --> 00:03:14,428
A Westinghouse motor drove the drum
through belts and three wheels

63
00:03:14,468 --> 00:03:17,758
of different sizes, with a
clutch to change direction.

64
00:03:18,188 --> 00:03:20,398
After eight turns the drum reversed,

65
00:03:20,648 --> 00:03:23,038
so the clothes did
not wad into a lump.

66
00:03:23,408 --> 00:03:25,878
It also had an emergency stop rod.

67
00:03:26,308 --> 00:03:29,598
The Thor was sold across the United
States from nineteen oh eight.

68
00:03:30,198 --> 00:03:32,368
Was the Thor the
first electric washer?

69
00:03:32,698 --> 00:03:33,598
Not everyone agrees.

70
00:03:33,638 --> 00:03:36,428
Another company, in Binghamton,

71
00:03:36,538 --> 00:03:38,958
New York, says it made
one in nineteen oh six,

72
00:03:38,998 --> 00:03:42,438
and a washing machine historian
has written that it cannot be

73
00:03:42,478 --> 00:03:45,488
concluded that Fisher
invented the electric washer.

74
00:03:45,878 --> 00:03:48,988
What we can say is that the
Thor was one of the first,

75
00:03:49,198 --> 00:03:52,045
and that The Henry Ford museum
keeps one from around nineteen

76
00:03:52,085 --> 00:03:52,948
oh seven.

77
00:03:59,533 --> 00:04:03,093
The electric washers of the nineteen
twenties still had a wringer

78
00:04:03,133 --> 00:04:03,863
on top.

79
00:04:04,333 --> 00:04:06,953
You fed each wet piece
between the rollers by hand,

80
00:04:06,993 --> 00:04:08,683
and mistakes were painful.

81
00:04:09,093 --> 00:04:10,883
That is where the phrase comes from.

82
00:04:11,466 --> 00:04:15,506
In nineteen twenty-two, Howard Snyder
of the Maytag Company developed

83
00:04:15,546 --> 00:04:19,236
an agitator that worked underwater
with a reversing motion.

84
00:04:19,706 --> 00:04:23,006
Until then, this type of washer
had not been able to compete

85
00:04:23,046 --> 00:04:24,196
with the drum machines.

86
00:04:24,746 --> 00:04:25,636
It could now.

87
00:04:37,118 --> 00:04:39,688
One estimate says that
by nineteen forty,

88
00:04:40,038 --> 00:04:43,578
about sixty percent of the
twenty-five million wired homes in

89
00:04:43,618 --> 00:04:46,368
the United States had an
electric washing machine.

90
00:04:46,718 --> 00:04:49,708
We could not confirm that
figure from a primary source,

91
00:04:49,898 --> 00:04:51,548
so treat it as an estimate.

92
00:04:52,064 --> 00:04:54,634
By then a washing
machine was not a luxury.

93
00:04:55,024 --> 00:04:58,294
It was an appliance that changed
what a day at home looked like.

94
00:04:58,464 --> 00:05:01,014
But somebody still had
to stand beside it:

95
00:05:01,164 --> 00:05:03,524
fill the tub, run the
clothes through the wringer,

96
00:05:03,564 --> 00:05:05,914
drain the water, and start the rinse.

97
00:05:16,609 --> 00:05:19,299
The goal was a machine that
did all of it by itself.

98
00:05:34,933 --> 00:05:38,873
In nineteen thirty-seven, Bendix
Home Appliances introduced what

99
00:05:38,913 --> 00:05:42,133
is widely called the first
automatic washing machine for the

100
00:05:42,173 --> 00:05:42,663
home.

101
00:05:42,953 --> 00:05:46,043
It loaded from the front,
like many washers today.

102
00:05:46,533 --> 00:05:50,243
It washed, rinsed and spun
the clothes dry on its own.

103
00:06:00,735 --> 00:06:02,245
The name came from a licence.

104
00:06:02,775 --> 00:06:06,255
A company called Avco borrowed
the Bendix name from an otherwise

105
00:06:06,295 --> 00:06:08,045
unrelated corporation.

106
00:06:08,335 --> 00:06:11,885
The machine itself looked a lot
like a front loader of today,

107
00:06:12,155 --> 00:06:14,065
and it had one famous problem.

108
00:06:14,295 --> 00:06:17,995
The drum had no suspension, so
the machine had to be fastened

109
00:06:18,035 --> 00:06:20,825
to the floor, or it would
walk across the room.

110
00:06:31,161 --> 00:06:33,071
Because Bendix got there first,

111
00:06:33,221 --> 00:06:36,561
one collector writes, it held
many of the patents for this kind

112
00:06:36,601 --> 00:06:41,011
of machine, so rivals had to pay
royalties or design around them.

113
00:06:41,381 --> 00:06:43,451
After the war, others followed.

114
00:06:43,761 --> 00:06:47,241
Sources and collectors give
nineteen forty-seven for General

115
00:06:47,281 --> 00:06:50,991
Electric, nineteen forty-eight
for Whirlpool's predecessor

116
00:06:51,341 --> 00:06:53,141
and nineteen forty-nine for Maytag,

117
00:06:53,181 --> 00:06:54,771
though dates vary.

118
00:06:55,381 --> 00:06:58,961
One collector site says Bendix
had built a million washers by

119
00:06:59,001 --> 00:07:00,191
nineteen forty-eight.

120
00:07:00,601 --> 00:07:02,391
We could not verify that number.

121
00:07:02,873 --> 00:07:05,913
The pictures in this chapter
come from a sponsored film made

122
00:07:05,953 --> 00:07:07,683
around nineteen fifty-two.

123
00:07:07,853 --> 00:07:10,803
It was a company film,
so it shows one brand,

124
00:07:10,913 --> 00:07:12,783
and that brand is not the point.

125
00:07:13,153 --> 00:07:16,173
It is a record of what people
were told a washing machine would

126
00:07:16,213 --> 00:07:17,783
give them: time.

127
00:07:27,582 --> 00:07:31,082
By one estimate, the average American
still spends about sixty-two

128
00:07:31,122 --> 00:07:32,792
hours a year on laundry.

129
00:07:33,042 --> 00:07:35,192
So the machine did not end laundry.

130
00:07:35,502 --> 00:07:37,242
It changed who stood beside it,

131
00:07:37,282 --> 00:07:38,432
and for how long.

132
00:07:39,033 --> 00:07:40,983
An early wringer gave us a phrase.

133
00:07:41,473 --> 00:07:45,763
A drum that reversed after eight
turns saved clothes from a lump.

134
00:07:46,293 --> 00:07:50,053
A machine that once had to be
bolted down now sits quietly in

135
00:07:50,093 --> 00:07:53,443
the corner of a kitchen, and
most of us never think about it.

136
00:07:53,929 --> 00:07:55,239
Ordinary objects.

137
00:07:55,349 --> 00:07:56,719
Extraordinary stories.
